course: dictionary
lesson: 14225
title: Namely: naming the exact things
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Sometimes a writer says a general word first, like "two colours", and then wants to tell you exactly which ones. The word namely does this job. It means "that is to say" or "in particular", and it is followed by the exact names.
explain: «We need two colours, namely red and blue.» Here "two colours" is the general idea, and namely tells you which two: red and blue. If you can swap in "that is to say" and the sentence still makes sense, namely is doing its job.
explain: Namely is often used in lists and in maths. «The factors of 6 are 1, 2, 3 and 6, namely the numbers that divide exactly into 6.» Namely comes just before the details it names, so when you see it, look for the list that follows.
fact: Namely means "that is to say" or "in particular".
fact: Namely is used before a list or a name to show exactly who or what you mean.
fact: Namely is an adverb, and it usually comes after a comma or a colon.
fact: Namely is a formal word, so you will see it more in writing and explanations than in everyday talk.
fact: A quick check is to swap in "that is to say" and see if the sentence still makes sense.
exercise: «The class has three pets, namely a rabbit, a hamster and a fish.» What does namely do here? | It shows the pets are sleeping | It says the pets are very big | It asks a question about the pets | *It names the exact pets
exercise: «The factors of 10, namely 1, 2, 5 and 10, all divide exactly into 10.» Which word tells you the list of names is coming? | factors | exactly | *namely | divide
exercise: Nadia says namely means "that is to say". Is she right? | No, it means "a long time ago" | No, it means "very quickly" | *Yes, you can swap namely for "that is to say" | No, it means "not at all"
test: «Ella packed a snack, namely an apple and some crackers.» What do the apple and the crackers show? | Where Ella lost her bag | *The exact snack Ella packed | The name of Ella's school | A snack that Ella did not like
test: Which word could replace namely in «a fruit, namely a banana»? | under | *that is to say | very | never
exam: Copy the word that introduces the exact names: «Our class visited a farm, namely Green Hill, last week.» | *namely | week | farm | class
